# Era 8 — Services Core (V26–V28)

_Phase 2 · Concept Layer V1_

**Status: ✅ done, verified 2026-09-17**

**Implementation note:** all three pages written new — `concepts/services.mdx` (V26, adapted
directly from the Era 2 V4 filled example, switched from the template's "acme-api" example
to the shared `paperclip`/`paperclip-db` vocabulary for consistency with every other page),
`concepts/service-databases-and-credentials.mdx` (V27), `concepts/service-clusters.mdx`
(V28). No `guides/services-workflow.mdx` exists yet (the example template's placeholder
link) — `services.mdx`'s own "Next steps" links to the CLI reference directly instead of a
non-existent guide, rather than leaving a broken link.

Covers T19–T21: Services, Service databases and credentials, Service clusters. This is the
exact worked example `concepts-explained.txt` itself uses ("For Services, users need to
understand what connecting PostgreSQL to their application accomplishes and when the
application can use it. They don't need the internal attachment schema, credential
provisioning mechanism, or network implementation.") — the Era 2 V4 template's filled
example is this topic, so this era should read closest to that reference example.

---

## V26 — Services (T19)

### Goal
New concept page: supported service types and versions, choosing a service, provisioning,
attaching applications, injected variables, prefixes, redeploy requirements, resizing,
upgrades, lifecycle actions.

### Reuses
`cli/service.mdx` (or its Era 7 split pages, per the sibling roadmap's own note that
`service`'s 46 subcommands will likely need more than one reference page) as the reference
target; `templates/concept-page-example.mdx` (Era 2 V4) as the direct pattern for this page,
since that template's worked example *is* Services.

### Implement
`concepts/services.mdx`: "what connecting PostgreSQL accomplishes" framed exactly as the
transcript's own example — injected variables as what the user's application receives (a
connection string appears as an environment variable), when the application can actually
use it (after a redeploy, if the attachment requires one — stated as a redeploy requirement,
not why), resizing/upgrades as user-triggered actions with stated consequences. Explicitly
excludes: attachment schema, credential provisioning mechanism, network implementation —
named directly in the transcript as the boundary for this exact topic.

### Validate
Direct comparison against the transcript's own worked instruction (lines 16–17) — this page
is graded against the user's own stated bar for it, not just the general disclosure test.

### Done when
Page exists and specifically satisfies the transcript's own worked example for this topic.

---

## V27 — Service databases and credentials (T20)

### Goal
New concept page: logical databases, application isolation, connection details, credential
reveal and rotation, detachment vs. access revocation, safe reassignment.

### Reuses
`cli/service.mdx`'s `database` subcommand family as reference target.

### Implement
`concepts/service-databases-and-credentials.mdx`: "logical database" explained as a
user-facing partition inside a service (isolation as an observable property: one
application's data isn't visible to another's), credential reveal/rotation as user actions
with stated consequences (rotating invalidates the old value — old connections may need to
reconnect), detachment vs. revocation as two distinct, clearly named user actions with
different blast radius (a natural fit for a `<Warning>` per `components.txt` on the
irreversible one). Checkpoint term kept out: `credential provisioning mechanism` (same one
named in the transcript for V26, applies here even more directly).

### Validate
Disclosure pass on credential handling specifically — confirm no mention of how credentials
are generated, stored, or transmitted internally, only what the user can see/do
(reveal/rotate/detach).

### Done when
Page exists; passes disclosure pass.

---

## V28 — Service clusters (T21)

### Goal
New concept page: when a cluster is useful, supported configurations, member health,
availability limitations, resizing, upgrades, replacing lost members, recovering partial
operations.

### Reuses
`cli/service.mdx`'s `cluster` subcommand family as reference target.

### Implement
`concepts/service-clusters.mdx`: "when a cluster is useful" as the opening problem/when
section (Invariant 3) rather than assuming the reader already wants one, member health as
observable per-member states, "replacing lost members" and "recovering partial operations"
as user-facing recovery procedures stated at outcome level (what the user runs/sees), not
the replication/consensus mechanism behind cluster health (an implicit checkpoint concern
even though not separately named in the addendum — flag it there if drafting surfaces
language needing it).

### Validate
Disclosure pass; if drafting finds cluster-health language needs a named checkpoint term not
yet in `DISCLOSURE-ADDENDUM.md`, add it there (Era 2's addendum is not assumed exhaustive —
this is the mechanism for extending it as real topics surface real risk).

### Done when
Page exists; addendum updated if a new checkpoint term was needed; closes Era 8.
